export const designingMultitenantSaas = "## Introduction\n\n**Multi-tenancy is the moment your product stops being an app and becomes a platform — and the moment a single missing `WHERE tenant_id = ?` can leak one customer's data to another.**\n\nI learned this building a live PropTech platform: a system where property managers, landlords, tenants (the human kind), and maintenance vendors all share one deployment but must never see each other's leases, payment records, or unit inspections. In that world \"tenant\" is doubly overloaded — the *account* (a property management company) and the *person renting a unit* — so I'll use **tenant** to mean the SaaS customer/account throughout, and call the renters \"residents.\"\n\nThis article is a distilled version of what actually held up in production: how to choose an isolation model, why Postgres Row-Level Security (RLS) beats hand-written `WHERE` clauses, how to layer role-based access on top, and the operational realities — provisioning, noisy neighbors, and compliance-grade data export — that nobody warns you about until you're paged at 2am.\n\n## The three isolation models\n\nEvery multi-tenant design lands on one of three physical layouts. The decision drives everything downstream: your migrations, your backups, your blast radius, and your unit economics.\n\n| Model | Isolation | Cost / density | Ops complexity | Best fit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared schema (`tenant_id` column) | Logical only | Highest density, cheapest | Low: one schema, one migration | Many small/medium tenants, usage-based pricing |\n| Schema-per-tenant | Namespace-level | Medium | Medium: N schemas to migrate | Hundreds of tenants, some customization |\n| Database-per-tenant | Physical | Lowest density, priciest | High: N databases, N backups | Few large/regulated/enterprise tenants |\n\n**Shared schema** puts every tenant's rows in the same tables, discriminated by a `tenant_id` column. It is the densest and cheapest — one connection pool, one set of indexes, one migration to run. The risk is that isolation is *purely logical*: it exists only as long as every query filters correctly. This is where RLS earns its keep.\n\n**Schema-per-tenant** gives each tenant its own Postgres schema (`tenant_042.leases`). You get cleaner separation and can `pg_dump` a single schema for export, but migrations must fan out across every schema, and Postgres degrades once you have thousands of tables (catalog bloat, autovacuum pressure, slow `\\d` and planning).\n\n**Database-per-tenant** is maximum isolation: separate databases (or even separate instances). Perfect for a regulated enterprise customer who demands physical separation or data residency in a specific region — but you now operate N databases with N migration runs and N backup schedules.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>These models aren't mutually exclusive. The pattern that scales best is <b>shared schema by default, promote to isolated database on demand</b>. Small tenants live cheaply in the shared pool; a large enterprise that signs a data-residency clause gets moved to a dedicated database — same application code, different connection string resolved per tenant.</p></div></div>\n\nFor the PropTech platform we ran shared-schema with RLS for the long tail of small agencies, and reserved database-per-tenant for two enterprise clients with contractual data-residency requirements.\n\n## Enforcing isolation with Postgres RLS\n\nIn a shared-schema design, isolation lives in exactly one place if you do it right: the database. Row-Level Security lets Postgres itself append a mandatory predicate to every query against a table — one that the application cannot forget or bypass.\n\nThe mechanism has three parts: a per-connection **tenant context**, a **policy** that reads it, and `ENABLE`/`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` on the table.\n\n```sql\n-- Every tenant-scoped table carries the discriminator, NOT NULL.\nCREATE TABLE leases (\n    id           uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n    tenant_id    uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(id),\n    unit_id      uuid NOT NULL,\n    resident_id  uuid NOT NULL,\n    rent_cents   integer NOT NULL,\n    status       text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',\n    created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Composite index leads with tenant_id: every RLS-filtered query uses it.\nCREATE INDEX idx_leases_tenant_status ON leases (tenant_id, status);\n\n-- Turn on RLS. FORCE applies it even to the table owner.\nALTER TABLE leases ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nALTER TABLE leases FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n\n-- The policy reads a session variable set per request.\nCREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON leases\n    USING      (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::uuid)\n    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::uuid);\n```\n\nTwo clauses matter. `USING` filters which rows are *visible* on `SELECT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`. `WITH CHECK` validates rows being *written* on `INSERT`/`UPDATE` — without it, a tenant could insert a row stamped with someone else's `tenant_id`. Specify both.\n\nThe context is a session variable set at the start of each request, inside the same transaction as the query:\n\n```ts\n// One connection is checked out per request from the pool.\n// set_config(key, value, is_local=true) scopes it to the transaction,\n// so it cannot leak to the next request that reuses this connection.\nasync function withTenant<T>(\n  pool: Pool,\n  tenantId: string,\n  fn: (client: PoolClient) => Promise<T>,\n): Promise<T> {\n  const client = await pool.connect();\n  try {\n    await client.query('BEGIN');\n    await client.query(\n      `SELECT set_config('app.current_tenant', $1, true)`,\n      [tenantId],\n    );\n    const result = await fn(client);\n    await client.query('COMMIT');\n    return result;\n  } catch (err) {\n    await client.query('ROLLBACK');\n    throw err;\n  } finally {\n    client.release(); // returns to pool; local setting already discarded\n  }\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Setting the tenant variable with <code>SET</code> (session-scoped) instead of <code>set_config(..., true)</code> (transaction-scoped) on a <b>pooled</b> connection. The variable survives after the request ends, and the next request that borrows that connection inherits a stale tenant — a silent cross-tenant leak. Always use the transaction-local form, and never run tenant queries as a <code>SUPERUSER</code> or <code>BYPASSRLS</code> role, which skips policies entirely.</p></div></div>\n\n## Why RLS beats application-level filtering\n\nThe naive alternative is to trust your code to add `WHERE tenant_id = $1` everywhere. That is the \"always filter by tenant_id\" discipline — and discipline is exactly the problem. It fails on:\n\n- The one report query written under deadline that forgets the clause.\n- A new join that pulls a second table nobody remembered to filter.\n- An ORM `findAll()` a junior engineer calls without a scope.\n- An analytics endpoint, a background job, a data migration script.\n\nEach is a single line away from exposing every tenant's data. RLS inverts the default: **queries return nothing unless the policy passes.** A forgotten filter now yields an *empty result*, not a leak — a bug that fails safe and is caught immediately in testing, rather than a breach discovered by a customer.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Request with JWT] --> B[Extract tenant_id and role]\n    B --> C[BEGIN and set app.current_tenant]\n    C --> D[App query without tenant filter]\n    D --> E[RLS appends tenant predicate]\n    E --> F[Return only this tenant rows]\n    C --> G[Forgot the WHERE clause]\n    G --> E\n```\n\nRLS is defense in depth, not a replacement for good code — but it's the only layer that catches the query you never wrote correctly. App-level filtering is still worth doing for clarity and index hints; just don't let it be the *only* thing standing between tenants.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>RLS predicates are ordinary conditions the planner sees, so they hit indexes normally — but only if <code>tenant_id</code> is the leading column of your indexes. Lead every composite index with <code>tenant_id</code> (<code>(tenant_id, status)</code>, <code>(tenant_id, created_at)</code>). Also, <code>current_setting()</code> is re-evaluated per row unless marked stable; wrapping it in a <code>STABLE</code> SQL function lets Postgres hoist it out of the row loop.</p></div></div>\n\n## Layering role-based access control\n\nTenant isolation answers *which* rows you can touch. RBAC answers *what* you can do with the rows inside your tenant. On the PropTech platform a property manager could edit leases, an accountant could read payments but not modify units, and a resident could see only their own lease.\n\nModel roles and permissions as data, not as `if` statements:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE roles (\n    id         uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n    tenant_id  uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(id),\n    name       text NOT NULL,                    -- 'manager', 'accountant'\n    UNIQUE (tenant_id, name)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE role_permissions (\n    role_id     uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES roles(id),\n    permission  text NOT NULL,                   -- 'lease:write', 'payment:read'\n    PRIMARY KEY (role_id, permission)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE user_roles (\n    user_id  uuid NOT NULL,\n    role_id  uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES roles(id),\n    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, role_id)\n);\n```\n\nKeep RBAC checks in the application layer (they map cleanly to HTTP 403 responses and are easy to unit-test), while RLS stays in the database for the hard isolation guarantee. But you can push especially sensitive rules into RLS too — for example, a resident should only see their *own* lease even within their tenant:\n\n```sql\n-- Managers/accountants see all tenant leases; residents see only their own.\nCREATE POLICY lease_access ON leases\n    USING (\n        tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::uuid\n        AND (\n            current_setting('app.role') IN ('manager', 'accountant')\n            OR resident_id = current_setting('app.current_user')::uuid\n        )\n    );\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-int\"><span class=\"ic\">🔥</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Interview question</b><p>\"If RLS already enforces isolation, why keep RBAC in the app?\" Because they answer different questions. RLS is a coarse, mandatory <i>row visibility</i> guarantee that must never be bypassable; RBAC is fine-grained <i>action authorization</i> that needs to return precise 403s, compose with feature flags, and be reasoned about in tests. Putting all of RBAC in SQL turns every permission tweak into a migration and buries your authorization logic where it's hard to audit.</p></div></div>\n\n## Tenant onboarding and provisioning\n\nEvery model needs provisioning to be *atomic and repeatable* — a half-created tenant is a support nightmare. In shared-schema, provisioning is mostly seeding a `tenants` row plus default roles; in database-per-tenant it's creating and migrating a whole database.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as Signup\n    participant API as Provisioning API\n    participant DB as Postgres\n    participant Q as Async worker\n    U->>API: Create tenant with plan\n    API->>DB: Insert tenant and default roles\n    API->>DB: Create admin user and grant role\n    API->>Q: Enqueue seed and welcome tasks\n    Q-->>API: Provisioning complete\n    API-->>U: Redirect to tenant workspace\n```\n\nThe rules that saved us:\n\n- **Wrap the synchronous part in one transaction.** Tenant row, default roles, first admin user, role grant — all commit together or not at all.\n- **Make it idempotent.** Key provisioning on the external signup/billing ID so a retried webhook doesn't create a duplicate tenant.\n- **Defer the slow parts.** Sample data, search index warm-up, and welcome emails go to a background queue; the user shouldn't wait on them.\n- **Store a per-tenant connection resolver.** Even in shared-schema, keep a `tenant → datasource` map so you can later migrate a tenant to a dedicated database without touching application code.\n\n## Noisy neighbors and per-tenant scaling\n\nShared infrastructure means one tenant's heavy report can starve everyone else. The failure mode is real: one property management company running a year-end export locks up the pool and the whole platform slows down.\n\nMitigations, roughly in order of effort:\n\n- **Per-tenant connection and rate limits.** Cap concurrent queries per tenant so no single account can monopolize the pool. A leaky-bucket limiter keyed by `tenant_id` at the API gateway is the cheapest win.\n- **Statement timeouts.** `SET statement_timeout` per request class kills runaway analytics queries before they cascade.\n- **Separate workloads by shape.** Route heavy reporting and exports to a read replica; keep the primary for transactional traffic.\n- **Track per-tenant cost.** Tag queries and rows so you know which tenants consume disproportionate CPU/storage — this feeds both capacity planning and pricing.\n- **Promote hot tenants.** When one tenant's usage dwarfs the rest, move them to a dedicated database. This is where the \"shared by default, isolate on demand\" architecture pays off.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Consider Postgres native partitioning by <code>tenant_id</code> (or hash of it) once your largest tables cross tens of millions of rows. Partition pruning lets a tenant-scoped query touch only that tenant's partition, shrinking index size and vacuum scope per tenant — and it makes eventually detaching a big tenant into its own database far less painful.</p></div></div>\n\n## Data export and compliance\n\nB2B customers will demand their data on exit, and regulators (GDPR, and for PropTech, financial-record retention rules) demand deletion and portability. Multi-tenancy makes both harder because the data is interleaved.\n\nDesign for this from day one:\n\n- **Export.** In shared-schema, a parameterized dump filtered by `tenant_id` across every table (respecting FK order) produces a clean, tenant-only archive. Schema-per-tenant makes this a trivial `pg_dump --schema=tenant_042`. Database-per-tenant is a full `pg_dump`. Note this asymmetry when you pick a model — export ergonomics correlate directly with isolation strength.\n- **Deletion / right to erasure.** With `ON DELETE CASCADE` from a central `tenants` row, deleting a tenant reliably removes every dependent row. Verify with a reconciliation query that no orphaned rows carry a deleted `tenant_id`.\n- **Audit trail.** Log every cross-tenant admin action (support staff impersonating a tenant, for instance) to an append-only table. Compliance auditors will ask \"who accessed this tenant's data and when,\" and you need an answer.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-rec\"><span class=\"ic\">🎯</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Recruiter insight</b><p>When you describe multi-tenant work on a resume or in an interview, lead with the <i>isolation guarantee and its blast radius</i>, not the framework. \"Enforced tenant isolation in Postgres with RLS so a forgotten filter fails closed\" signals security-conscious senior thinking far more than \"built a SaaS with Node and Postgres.\"</p></div></div>\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>Pick an isolation model by blast radius and tenant profile: shared schema for density, database-per-tenant for regulated enterprise, and design so you can promote a tenant from one to the other.</li>\n<li>In shared-schema, enforce isolation in the database with RLS — a forgotten filter should return zero rows, not leak data.</li>\n<li>Set the tenant context with transaction-local <code>set_config(..., true)</code> and always specify both <code>USING</code> and <code>WITH CHECK</code>; never query as a <code>BYPASSRLS</code> role.</li>\n<li>Layer RBAC in the application for precise action authorization; keep RLS for coarse, unbypassable row visibility.</li>\n<li>Lead every index with <code>tenant_id</code>, and defend against noisy neighbors with per-tenant rate limits, statement timeouts, and read replicas.</li>\n<li>Build export, cascade deletion, and cross-tenant audit logging in from day one — compliance is not a bolt-on.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Does RLS add significant query overhead?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Minimal, if indexed correctly. The policy predicate is just another condition the planner evaluates, and it uses your <code>tenant_id</code>-leading indexes normally. The main pitfall is re-evaluating <code>current_setting()</code> per row — wrap it in a <code>STABLE</code> function so Postgres hoists it out of the loop. In practice the safety gain vastly outweighs the sub-millisecond cost.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Can I mix isolation models in one product?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Yes, and it's often the best answer. Run most tenants in a shared schema for cost efficiency, and provision dedicated databases only for enterprise or regulated customers. The key is a per-tenant connection resolver so the application code is identical regardless of where a tenant physically lives.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>How do I safely let support staff access a tenant's data?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Use scoped impersonation: a support session explicitly sets <code>app.current_tenant</code> to the target tenant, and every such action is written to an append-only audit log with the staff member's identity. Never grant support a <code>BYPASSRLS</code> role — that removes the very guarantee you built.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>What happens if the tenant context variable is never set?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>With a policy like <code>tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::uuid</code>, an unset variable raises an error rather than matching everything — which is the fail-safe you want. To be explicit, use <code>current_setting('app.current_tenant', true)</code> (which returns NULL when missing) only if your policy treats NULL as \"match nothing,\" and add a connection-level guard that rejects tenant-scoped queries when no context is set.</p></div></details>\n";
